      "reasons": [
        "The packet does not support the claim that illusory contours are “perceived edge or shape… even though no actual contour is physically drawn.” It only mentions “illusory contours (Kanizsa shapes)” and an “inducing context,” without stating anything about the physical absence of contours.",
        "The definition’s statement that it “depends on surrounding visual context that induces the impression of a boundary or form” is only partially supported: the packet supports the idea of an “inducing context,” but it does not describe the dependence in terms of surrounding visual context producing a “boundary or form.”",
        "The definition extends beyond the packet by adding specifics about how the visual system experiences the contour (“perceived edge or shape… boundary or form”) that are not explicitly described in the provided text."
